#basePath TestChapter

:: Preload
@function    ChapterPreload

$call SetBackground("bg/Room.png")

// #region Bedroom 

:: Start
@trigger    ChapterStart
@cutscene

{@}   
       The Mid-West Regional DNR Headquarters. 
       You've been working here for a few months now.
       Mostly, you've been doing paper work. 
       While you work hard on it, it's quite... boring work. 
       You see your boss come to your desk. 

{@Boss}
        Happy Monday! 
        I got something for ya. 

{@}
        You anticipate more papers to hit your desk. 
        Instead, it's a pair of car keys. 

{@Boss} 
        You're going into the field today. 

{@Player}
        I am? 

{@Boss}
        Bout time we put your expertise to use. 
        Got reports of some weird happenings with the fish populations in Mittenson. 
        Barter Lake area, you heard of it?

{@}
        Barter Lake. 
        You've heard about this region. 
        You've always wanted to visit. 
        Getting paid for it seems like a valid enough reason. 
        And it would beat being in a stuffy office building anyday.  

{@Boss} 
        I have some friends who're fishing guides.
        Normally we'd have you be accompanied by someone. 
        However, we're a bit strained at the moment and, well you've been making good progress. 
        Think of it as a trial run. 
        

{@}
        You find yourself eager for something new. 
        You grab the car keys. 


// #endregion 

// #region Docks
 
$goto .TravelDocks

:: TravelDocks

{@}     
       You make your way into the lovely town of Mittenson. 
       Leading into town are an accompaniment of Sugar Maples and Birches. 
       It's May after all. 
       The trees are finally getting back their color from a sleepy winter. 
       As you make your way to the docks, you spot two fishing guides. 

        
$choice .Docks.A; Guess I should go ask those guides. 

:: .Docks.A

{@} 
        You walk towards them.

$goto .AnglersDocks

:: AnglersDocks


{@Angler1}      
        Odd isn't?
{@Angler2}      
        Feels like they're just disappearin'...

{@Player} 
        Hiya! 

{@}
        The anglers turn your way. 

{@Angler1}
        Can I help you? 

{@Player}
        I'm with the DNR, the Boss sent me. 

{@Angler1} 
        Oh! Right! Well welcome to Mittenson! 
        
{@Angler2}
        Maybe you can help us.

{@Player} 
        How so? 

{@Angler1}
        We've... been noticing a troubling trend. 

{@Angler2} 
        We give fishing tours on the lake, it's a big business here. 

{@Angler1}
        Thing is... the Lake Trout...

{@Angler2} 
        There's hardly any. We ususally catch a few a day but now, well we haven't seen any. 

{@Angler1} 
        And we were hoping you could figure out why. 

{@Player} 
        I see... how long has this been happening?  

{@Angler2}
        Maybe... the past month or two? 
        The populations take a bit to adjust due to winter.
        But it's like they... for the most part, disappeared. 

{@Player}
        Just to confirm, you would say the fish population has decreased from last year? 

{@Angler1}     
        Yep. 

$call GiveEvidenceCard("Evidence-Decreasing-Fish-Populations")
     
{@Player} 
        This... is concerning. Thanks for your help, gentlemen. 

{@Angler2}
        Any idea to what it could be?

{@Player}
        I need to get some more information on what's going on.
        So... no.  
        But, the town needs to be aware. I'm heading to Town Hall. 

{@Angler1}
        Yea, best that they know. 

{@Player}
        See you gentlemen! Nice meeting you! 

{@}
        The anglers nod their heads and wave as you start to head away from the docks. 
        Decreasing fish populations at Barter Lake. 
        You think back to your college days and remember a case study mentioning Barter Lake and the town of Mittenson. 
        You can't seem to recall it. 
        Anyways, best you go to Town Hall. 

$choice .Town.Hall.A; Time to notify the mayor. 

:: Town.Hall.A

{@}
        You get into your car and make your way towards the town center.
        
$goto .Town.Hall.Location.A

//endregion 

// #region Town Hall (A)

:: Town.Hall.Location.A 

{@}     You walk towards the Town Hall. 
        You enter the building and it just so happens the Mayor is standing in the lobby. 
        How convenient. 

{@Player}      
        Madam Mayor!

{@Mayor} 
        Hi! Do... I know you? 

{@Player} 
        I'm with the DNR. I'm investigating the rumors regarding the decrease in Lake Trout population in Barter Lake. 

{@Mayor} 
        I have heard about that... listen I'm very busy, maybe we can meet-- 

{@Player} 
        I understand Madam Mayor, but I only intend to notify you of this.
        Whatever is going on, it seems serious. 

{@Mayor}
        I see... okay. Well. Thank you. 
        I'm gonna be honest here. 
        It's gonna take some convincing to consider this a real issue. 
        It's not up to me, rather the council. 
        If you're able to find enough evidence on what's going on, and even have a plan, the council may be willing to help. 
        We're having a council meeting this Friday. 
        I'd advise going to the Nature Center or the Fish Hatchery. 
 
$choice .Nature.Center.A; The Nature Center
$choice .Fish.Hatchery.A; The Fish Hatchery
$choose branch 

:: Nature.Center.A

{@Player} 
        I'll check out the Nature Center. 

{@Mayor} 
        The Nature Center is a great source! 

$goto .Nature.Center.Location.A 

:: Fish.Hatchery.A

{@Player} The Hatchery probably knows best. 

{@Mayor} Best of luck!

$goto .Fish.Hatchery.Location.A

//endregion

// #region Museum 

:: Nature.Center.Location.A

{@} 
        You make your way to the Nature Center.
        It feels like no one is here. 
        In fact, you don't notice anyone until a short Nature Center Educator shifts slightly at the front desk. 
        They're reading a book. It's titled <i>Aquatic Species: Native and Invasive<i> 
        They speak to you, still reading their book. 

{@Curator}
        Welcome to the Nature Center. Tours on the right. Gift shop on the left. Have fun. 

{@Player} 
        Hi. Actually, I'm with the DNR, I was wondering if I could learn more about the Barter Lake region. 
         

{@}            
        The Educator's eyes glance up at you for the first time. They light up. 

{@Curator} 
        You want to learn about the Barter Lake Region?
        

{@Player} 
        Uh... yes-- 

{@} 
        The Educator shoots up, digs under their desk and lays out several documents and books. 
        The Educator has an eager grin on their face. 

{@Curator} 
        What do you want to know? 

$choice .Decreasing.Fish.Pop.A; Could you tell me more about the decreasing fish population? 

:: Decreasing.Fish.Pop.A

{@Curator}
        Mittenson isn't a stranger to flucuating fishing populations. 
        When commericial fishing boomed in the 50s and 60s, populations fell dramatically. 
        Once commericial fishing was phased out, the population bounced back. 
        Though this recent trend is unknown. Which is why I have a theory. 

{@Player}
        A theory? 

{@Curator}
        I believe an invasive species is to blame. 

{@Player}
        I see... and which one. 

{@Curator}
        I-... well--

{@}     
        They're phone buzzes. It's a text message with a photo attached. 

{@Curator}
        That's my friend, they're a field tech with the Research Lab. 
        ...
        We may have just gotten our answer. 


{@}     The Educator holds up their phone to you to show a Lake Trout with a circular bite on it's side. 

{@Player} 
        Sea Lamprey... 

$call GiveEvidenceCard ("Evidence-Lamprey-Discovered")

$choice .Lamprey.Origin.A; Where are they coming from?
$choice .Lamprey.Invasive.A; Have there been Lamprey in this region to this degree before?  
$choice .Lamprey.Stop; Where was this?  
$choose branch 

:: Lamprey.Origin.A

{@Curator} 
        As you may know, Sea Lamprey originated from the canals that lead to the Atalantic. 
        They were introduced to the region when shipping routes were made to bypass Niagra Falls.
        Most of those tributaries stem from the East into the lake. 

$choice .Lamprey.Invasive.B; What else can you tell me about Sea Lamprey in this region? 
$choice .Lamprey.Stop; Where was this photo taken? 
$choose branch

:: Lamprey.Origin.B

{@Curator}     
        They came from the canals that lead to the Atlantic, as you know.
        They only became a problem when those shipping canals were created. 
        Majority of the tributaries are on the Eastern side of the lake. 

$choice .Lamprey.Stop; Where's your friend? 

:: Lamprey.Invasive.A

{@Curator}
        Since Sea Lamprey are parasitic, we've closely montiored their population since they were first introduced in the 80s. 
        We were able to manage it then, but a lot of people have long forgotten that they were a threat in the first place. 

$choice .Lamprey.Origin.B; Where did they come from? 
$choice .Lamprey.Stop; I need to know where your friend is. 
$choose branch

:: Lamprey.Invasive.B 

{@Curator} 
        Since Sea Lamprey are parasitic, we've closely montiored their population since they were first introduced in the 80s. 
        We were able to manage it then, but a lot of people have long forgotten that they were a threat in the first place.

$choice .Lamprey.Stop; Thanks, could you tell me where this photo was taken? 

:: Lamprey.Stop 

{@Curator} 
        My friend works at the Field Station by the Tributary.
        I also have friends at the University Research Lab and the Corps of Engineers. 
        They also may be able to help! 

$choice .Field.Station.Trib.A; I should talk to your friend. 
$choice .University.Research.Lab.A; I'm going to head to the University Research Lab. 
$choice .Corps.Engineers.A; The Corps may have some insight into this.
$choose branch 

:: University.Research.Lab.A 

{@} 
        You make your way to the University Research Lab. 

$goto .University.Research.Lab.Location.A 

:: Field.Station.Trib.A

{@} 
        You make your way to the Field Station. 

$goto .Field.Station.Trib.Location.A

:: Corps.Engineers.A 

{@} 
        You make your way to the Army Corps of Engineers Office. 


//endregion 

// #region Fish Hatchery

:: Fish.Hatchery.Location.A

{@} 
        You make your way to the Mittenson Fish Hatchery.
        

{@Biologist}
        Hi there! How can I help you? 

{@Player} 
        I'm with the DNR. I was hoping to get some more information regarding the fish populations.  

{@Biologist}
        Yep... we've noticed the decrease as well.  

{@Player}
        You have any idea as to why that may be? 

{@Biologist}
        Actually... yes. Take a look.

{@}
        The biologist take our their phone. They show several Lake Trout with circular bite marks. 

{@Player}      
        Sea Lamprey...

$call GiveEvidenceCard ("Evidence-Lamprey-Discovered")
       
{@Biologist} 
        We might be able to help. 
        We do have a few Sterile Sea Lamprey on hand, though we will need more time to collect more. 
        We can help out as best as we can. 

$call GiveEvidenceCard ("Evidence-Sterile-Lamprey-Discovered")

{@Player} 
        Thanks. 
        In the mean time, I should go somewhere else to learn more on what's going on. 

$choice .DNR.Office.A; The DNR Office
$choice .Field.Station.Trib.B; The Field Station
$choice .University.Research.Lab.A; The University Research Lab
$choose branch 


:: DNR.Office.A 

{@} 
        You make your way to the DNR Office. 

$goto .DNR.Office.Location.A

:: Field.Station.Trib.B

{@}
        You make your way to the Field Station

$goto .Field.Station.Trib.Location.B

:: University.Research.Lab.B

{@}     
        You make your way to the University Research Lab. 

$goto .University.Research.Lab.Location.A


//endregion 

//#region Field Station Trib A 

:: Field.Station.Trib.Location.A

{@} 
        You find yourself at the tributary. 
        The sound of the rushing water is accompanied alongside bird-sung melodies. 
        As you traverse the rocky stream, you spot two field techs.

        You decide to approach. 

{@FieldTech1} 
        Do you think they'll even approve it? 

{@FieldTech2}
        Hope so... otherwise we're gonna run into some major issues. 

{@Player}
        Hi! 


//endregion 

//#region Univeristy Research Lab

:: University.Research.Lab.Location.A 

{@} 
        As you reach the lab, a researcher clacks away at his computer. 

{@Researcher}
        Hi there! How can I help you? 

{@Player} 
        I was hoping to get some more information regarding Sea Lamprey. 

{@Researcher}
        You're in the right place! Anything in particular? 

$choice .Lamprey.Bites.B; Can you confirm that these are Sea Lamprey bites. 
$choice .Lamprey.Pheremones; How might you approach stopping Sea Lamprey? 
$choose branch 

{@Researcher} 
        They're effective! Since you work with the DNR, would you be willing to coordinate with them for us? 

$choice .DNR.Office.B; I can do that!
$choice .Field.Station.Trib.C; I need to go check something out first. 
$choose branch 
 
:: Lamprey.Bites.B

{@}     
        You show the Researcher the photo you took.

{@Researcher} 
        Yes... very concerning. Seems like the Sea Lamprey in our region are getting worse. 

$choice .Lamprey.Pheromones; What preventatives have you been researching?

:: Lamprey.Pheromones

{@Researcher} 
        Well, we're studying the use pheromones as a lure. 
        Sea Lamprey are sensitive to all sorts of smells, especially their own pheremones to mate. 
        So, using their pheromones to attract them make them easy to trap. 
        Best to trap them before they head upstream to spawn. 

{@Player}
        So pheromones could be a real effective tool against the Sea Lamprey... 

$call GiveEvidenceCard("Evidence-Pheromones")

:: DNR.Office.B

{@}
        You make your way to the local DNR Office 

$goto .DNR.Office.Location.A


:: Field.Station.Trib.C 

{@}
        You make your way to the Field Station. 

$goto .Field.Station.Trib.Location.A